      "key": "prime_realisation_law",
      "statement": "Derive Prime atoms from a logically complete candidate Ontology before proposing top-level units; whole-family Prime contributes evidence to Ontology acceptance. Target design, IACS, modules, functions, schemas and tests project accepted atoms and do not originate Prime meaning. Typed closure alone does not justify a new top-level type. Prime-conformance requires projection of accepted atoms without introducing, duplicating or reinterpreting semantic authority; topological/effect boundaries first receive atom derivation and acceptance. Typical lawful reasons include new feature topology/work-graph branch, typed-carrier transformation, effect, validation/admission or projection boundary. A unit existing only to shorten a call site without a semantic boundary, aggregate unrelated behaviour, rename an old interface, shuttle through wrappers, hide mutation/fallback/orchestration, name authority-free payload variation, expose internal records, mirror a carrier with slight field differences or relieve typing discomfort by multiplying peers is non-conformant. Prefer composition, existing lawful functions, sharper/fewer boundaries and carrier families; the goal is preventing semantic fragmentation and helper sprawl, not minimising lines.",
